NNAAMMEE
     eeiiggrrppdd..ccoonnff — EIGRP routing daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The eigrpd(8) daemon implements the Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing
     Protocol.

     The eeiiggrrppdd..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for eigrpd(8).

     _R_o_u_t_i_n_g _i_n_s_t_a_n_c_e_s
           Multiple routing instances can be defined.  Routing instances are
           defined hierarchically by address-family and then autonomous-sys‐
           tem.

     _I_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_s _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Interface-specific parameters.

     Argument names not beginning with a letter, digit, or underscore must be
     quoted.

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword,
     for example:

           include "/etc/eigrpd.sub.conf"

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, bbaannddwwiiddtthh, iinntteerrffaaccee, or hheelllloo--iinntteerrvvaall).  Within un‐
     quoted arguments, the string $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           fastethernet="100000"
           address-family ipv4 {
                   autonomous-system 1 {
                           interface em1 {
                                   bandwidth $fastethernet
                           }
                   }
           }

     The same can be accomplished by specifying the bandwidth globally or
     within the address-family or autonomous-system declaration.  _G_l_o_b_a_l
     _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n Several settings can be configured globally, per address-
     family, per autonomous-system and per interface.  The only settings that
     can be set globally and not overruled are listed below.

     ffiibb--pprriioorriittyy--iinntteerrnnaall _p_r_i_o
             Set the routing priority of EIGRP internal routes to _p_r_i_o.  The
             default is 28.

     ffiibb--pprriioorriittyy--eexxtteerrnnaall _p_r_i_o
             Set the routing priority of EIGRP external routes to _p_r_i_o.  This
             option may be used as a simple loop-prevention mechanism when an‐
             other routing protocol is being redistributed into EIGRP.  The
             default is 28.

     ffiibb--pprriioorriittyy--ssuummmmaarryy _p_r_i_o
             Set the routing priority of EIGRP summary routes to _p_r_i_o.  The
             default is 28.

     ffiibb--uuppddaattee (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to nnoo, do not update the Forwarding Information Base,
             a.k.a. the kernel routing table.  The default is yyeess.

     rrddoommaaiinn _t_a_b_l_e_i_d
             Specifies the routing table eigrpd(8) should modify.  Table 0 is
             the default table.

     rroouutteerr--iidd _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
             Set the router ID; if not specified, the numerically lowest IP
             address of the router will be used.

   RRoouuttiinngg iinnssttaanncceess
     Multiple routing instances can be defined.  Routing instances are defined
     hierarchically by address-family and then autonomous-system.

           address-family ipv4 {
                   ...
                   autonomous-system 1 {
                           ...
                           interface em0 {
                                   ...
                           }
                   }
           }

     Routing-instance specific parameters are listed below.

     aaccttiivvee--ttiimmeeoouutt _m_i_n_u_t_e_s
             Set the maximum time to wait before declaring a route to be in
             the stuck in active state.  If 0 is given, the active timeout is
             disabled.  The default value is 3; valid range is 0-65535.

     aaddddrreessss--ffaammiillyy (iippvv44|iippvv66)
             Specify an address-family section, grouping one or more autono‐
             mous-systems.

     aauuttoonnoommoouuss--ssyysstteemm _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Specify the autonomous-system, grouping one or more interfaces.
             Valid range is 1-65535.

     ddeeffaauulltt--mmeettrriicc _b_a_n_d_w_i_d_t_h _d_e_l_a_y _r_e_l_i_a_b_i_l_i_t_y _l_o_a_d _m_t_u
             Specify a default metric for all routes redistributed into EIGRP.
             Valid ranges are: 1-10000000 for the bandwidth, 1-16777215 for
             the delay, 1-255 for the reliability, 1-255 for the load and
             1-65535 for the mtu.

     kk--vvaalluueess _K_1 _K_2 _K_3 _K_4 _K_5 _K_6
             Set the coefficients used by the composite metric calculation.
             Two routers become neighbors only if their K-values are the same.
             For K1 and K3, The default value is 1.  For K2, K4, K5 and K6 the
             default value is 0; valid range is 1-254.

     mmaaxxiimmuumm--hhooppss _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Advertise as unreachable the routes with a hop count higher than
             specified.  The default value is 100; valid range is 1-255.

     mmaaxxiimmuumm--ppaatthhss _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Specify the maximum number of ECMP paths to be installed in the
             FIB for each route.  The default value is 4; valid range is 1-32.

     [nnoo] rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee (ssttaattiicc|ccoonnnneecctteedd|oossppff|rriipp|ddeeffaauulltt) [mmeettrriicc _b_a_n_d_w_i_d_t_h
             _d_e_l_a_y _r_e_l_i_a_b_i_l_i_t_y _l_o_a_d _m_t_u]

     [nnoo] rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee _p_r_e_f_i_x [mmeettrriicc _b_a_n_d_w_i_d_t_h _d_e_l_a_y _r_e_l_i_a_b_i_l_i_t_y _l_o_a_d _m_t_u]
             If set to ccoonnnneecctteedd, routes to directly attached networks will be
             announced over EIGRP.  If set to ssttaattiicc, static routes will be
             announced over EIGRP.  If set to oossppff, OSPF routes will be an‐
             nounced over EIGRP.  If set to rriipp, RIP routes will be announced
             over EIGRP.  If set to ddeeffaauulltt, a default route pointing to this
             router will be announced over EIGRP.  It is possible to specify a
             network range with _p_r_e_f_i_x; networks need to be part of that range
             to be redistributed.  By default no additional routes will be an‐
             nounced over EIGRP.

             rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee statements are evaluated in sequential order, from
             first to last.  The first matching rule decides if a route should
             be redistributed or not.  Matching rules starting with nnoo will
             force the route to be not announced.  The only exception is
             ddeeffaauulltt, which will be set no matter what, and additionally nnoo
             cannot be used together with it.

             It is possible to set the route mmeettrriicc for each redistribute
             rule.

     vvaarriiaannccee _m_u_l_t_i_p_l_i_e_r
             Set the variance used to permit the installation of feasible suc‐
             cessors in the FIB if their metric is lower than the metric of
             the successor multiplied by the specified multiplier.  The de‐
             fault value is 1; valid range is 1-128.

   IInntteerrffaacceess ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Each interface can have several parameters configured individually, oth‐
     erwise they are inherited.  Interfaces can pertain to multiple routing
     instances.  An interface is specified by its name.

           interface em0 {
                   ...
           }

     Interface-specific parameters are listed below.

     bbaannddwwiiddtthh _b_a_n_d_w_i_d_t_h
             Set the interface bandwidth in kilobits per second.  The band‐
             width is used as part of the EIGRP composite metric.  The default
             value is 100000; valid range is 1-10000000.

     ddeellaayy _d_e_l_a_y
             Set the interface delay in tens of microseconds.  The delay is
             used as part of the EIGRP composite metric.  The default value is
             10; valid range is 1-16777215.

     hheelllloo--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the hello interval.  The default value is 5; valid range is
             1-65535 seconds.

     hhoollddttiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the hello holdtime.  The default value is 15; valid range is
             1-65535 seconds.

     ppaassssiivvee
             Prevent transmission and reception of EIGRP packets on this in‐
             terface.

     sspplliitt--hhoorriizzoonn (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to nnoo, the split horizon rule will be disabled on this in‐
             terface.  This option should be used with caution since it can
             introduce routing loops in point-to-point or broadcast networks.
             The default is yyeess.

     ssuummmmaarryy--aaddddrreessss _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n
             Configure a summary aggregate address for this interface.  Multi‐
             ple summary addresses can be configured.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/eigrpd.conf           eigrpd(8) configuration file.
     /etc/examples/eigrpd.conf  Example configuration file.

